## 1 Scope and closed decisions

### 1.1 Player loop

1. Accept a Lodeworks campaign contract at Mission Control.
2. Launch a hab and deploy it on a suitable landed site.
3. Place the hab's included starter buildings on its deck, with no loose construction cargo requirement.
4. Use local production to pay material costs for extra storage, buildings and upgrades.
5. Inspect the deposit; mine ore into graded batches and optionally process it.
6. Establish manual shipments or automated routes to other colonies or KSC.
7. Supply enough qualifying cargo to the contract's receiving endpoint.
8. Complete the contract and receive funds, reputation, and permanent blueprints.
9. Use larger storage, faster equipment, and more capable transports to expand the network.

Interesting constraints are extraction rate, grade, deposit life, power, storage, fuel production, propellant mass, vehicle range, depot placement, transport capacity, journey time, maintenance, and campaign objectives. Avoid repetitive colony switching as a requirement to keep ordinary operations running.

### 1.2 Mandatory decisions

- One colony has one deployed hab part as its physical anchor. Additional buildings and shipment vehicles are cosmetic GameObjects, not vessels.
- Colony and depot cargo and transport propellants live only in the scenario-owned ledger. Do not mirror them into stock resource tanks. Explicit manual stock-fuel bridge transfers are allowed under section 9.6, with exactly one inventory owner before and after each transfer.
- Colonies use their own explicit power system, specified in section 7. Colony power is not stock ElectricCharge. This intentionally replaces the old unloaded-EC restriction and RequestResource factory implementation.
- Stock solar panels, batteries, fuel cells, ISRU, and stock Ore remain unchanged. They operate normally on spacecraft and do not silently power colony machinery. Transport uses the original stock LiquidFuel/Oxidizer IDs and their stock metadata, never a custom finished propellant; fuel-feed is only a raw production ingredient.
- Shipment matter leaves source inventory incrementally into a single loading escrow/onboard owner under section 4.3. Departure commits the loaded plan/location atomically, not a second cargo removal. Animations never own cargo or change arrival time.
- Automated routes are authoritative gameplay. Cosmetic trucks remain pictures of local activity; local transfers are instantaneous.
- First deployment has one finite prefabricated starter set included with each hab. Extra buildings and upgrades consume the endpoint's Metal and, where specified, Machine Parts. No ongoing building placement funds fee substitutes for these materials. All advanced construction needs its blueprint; supplied starter pieces are baseline exceptions defined explicitly below. Funds still pay real parts, vehicle commissioning, transport services and emergency repairs.
- The campaign uses deliveries and event counters, never inventory subtraction from acceptance-time snapshots.
- KSC is a permanent abstract receiving endpoint, not a vessel or a colony. It provides neither remote mining nor an unlimited remote power source. KSC may sell fuel to a transport physically present there, through an explicit local purchase; it cannot remotely refill colonies or depots.
- Preserve ore provenance through mining, stacking, processing selection, shipments, and quest validation.
- No required life support, radiation, city population, physical traffic, terrain flattening, or multiplayer.
- No kOS, kRPC, Kerbalism, MKS, Kopernicus, or Kerbal Konstructs dependency. Unknown celestial bodies receive a default profile.
- Orbital depots are in this release. The player launches a real depot-core part on an ordinary stock vessel and registers a valid orbit. Cargo transport models remain cosmetic. Stock vessel construction is deferred.

### 1.3 Modes

Career is the reference campaign mode. Science mode has campaign blueprints and contracts only if the verified stock contract integration supports it; otherwise use the same quest records through the campaign window, with no funds or reputation transaction. Sandbox has every blueprint available by default and zero funds costs, while power, inventory, material construction costs, transport, and optional wear still operate. Sandbox also supplies the same starter kit; optional debug grants are explicit and campaign-ineligible. Offer a sandbox campaign challenge toggle using the campaign window, without pretending Mission Control exists there.

Clean Phase 1 evidence records stock contracts unavailable in science/sandbox; use the campaign window there unless new verified evidence explicitly amends that finding. Section 17.1 is the complete economy/mode matrix: science and sandbox record zero-valued funds receipts without touching Funding, with no independent currency or reputation. Free local KSC fuel in these modes is still a located purchase/transfer; remote endpoints must produce or receive fuel. Paid-basis retirement is zero for free hulls. Mode changes cannot mint refunds for hulls that never incurred a stock charge.

## 2 Architecture and ownership

Use C# 9, net48 for the KSP plugin, dual-target netstandard2.0/net48 for the pure simulation, and net8.0 for tests. Package only the net48 simulation build for KSP, preserving the accepted Phase 1 runtime-load repair; tests use netstandard2.0. Verify target runtime compatibility before building features.

```text
Lodeworks.Sim
  InventoryMath, ReservationBook, AllocationPolicy, RecipeMath, ExtractionMath, PowerMath, WearMath, FaultMath
  FuelMath, ManeuverBudget, TransportPlanner, DepotSimulator
  ShipmentMath, RouteScheduler, DispatchPolicy, BerthScheduler, WaitGraph
  NetworkDiagnostics, NetworkForecast, ColonySimulator, WorldSimulator
  CampaignEvaluator, deterministic hashes, config validation, schema migration
Lodeworks.Ksp
  LodeworksScenario, LodeworksHab, LodeworksDepotCore, endpoint discovery, contracts
  BlueprintAdapter, celestial environment adapter, UI, visuals, save adapter
GameData/Lodeworks
  Parts, Resources, Recipes, Buildings, BodyProfiles, Campaign, Transport
  Localization, Icons, Settings, version metadata
```

Ship both Lodeworks.dll and Lodeworks.Sim.dll. Never ship KSP/Unity assemblies or test dependencies. Pin build dependencies and record their versions. KSPBuildTools is a build dependency only; verify its packaging behavior for both output assemblies.

The scenario owns colonies, orbital depots, buildings, batches, veins, fuel-feed reservoirs, routes, located vehicles and their onboard propellant, shipments, campaign progress, unlocks, and simulation clocks. The hab persists only anchor identity and deploy state needed to reconnect to scenario records. Never have competing converter state on both a PartModule and a building record.

KSP integration gathers validated environment and crew snapshots, advances the pure simulation, and presents resulting state. UI commands use the same transaction methods as automated commands. Never modify inventory directly from IMGUI drawing or an animation callback.

Use stable colony, building, route, depot, vehicle, transport-slot, shipment, contract-instance, and event IDs. Vessel GUIDs are references, not colony identity. Use invariant serialization and deterministic ordering by stable ID.

## 3 Colonies and placement

### 3.1 Deployment

The hab is lw-hab-250. It has no crew seats; a stock crewed part attached to the same vessel supplies crew. Crew efficiency is applied to processing and extraction, not autonomous transport or unmanned depot transfer operations.

Each hab carries a persisted starter-kit inventory and immutable grant receipt tied to the actual part identity. This is prefabricated equipment, not seed ore or free fuel. Grant once per newly launched hab after valid deployment, never once per scene/colony ID; moving or redeploying the same hab carries its remaining kit state rather than resetting it. A newly launched replacement hab is a genuinely new paid part and may have its own kit.

Create a colony only after an explicit Deploy Colony action on a landed vessel. Refuse prelaunch, orbiting, flying, splashed, Sun, and terrainless-body deployment. Show the actual reason. Do not award seed inventory in the shipped configuration.

Measure terrain and validate placement before committing the colony. Deployment cannot first create inventory and later discover that the site is invalid. Store bodyName, latitude, normalized longitude, heading, anchor part identity, and colonyId. The cell and surveyed site remain fixed after founding.

Allow one deployed colony anchor per vessel. Refuse deployment of a second anchor on that vessel. If docking joins two already deployed colonies, suspend industry and transport departures for both until separation restores valid anchors; do not merge or delete ledgers. Remap vessel references using anchor part identity after docking/undocking.

If an anchor is no longer landed or moves more than 20 m from its saved site, suspend extraction, production, and departure. Preserve inventory. Resuming requires return within 20 m and a valid landed state, or explicit relocation. A pushed hab cannot mine the previous cell from elsewhere.

### 3.2 Deck

Use a 24 m square deck with a 5 by 5 placement grid; grid indices -2 through 2, spacing 4 m. The hab occupies 0,0. One building per remaining slot. Cosmetic models must fit their footprint. A transport terminal occupies a grid slot and contains the pad visual; departing models do not require an additional vessel.

Convert corner offsets to geographic coordinates using stored heading. Raycast terrain first, excluding vessels and Lodeworks objects; use a verified PQS height query as fallback. Retry up to ten frames after load for missing terrain colliders. Place deck altitude 1.5 m above the highest corner. Reject new sites with corner height difference over 8 m or atan(heightDifference / 24 m) over 15 degrees. Cap visible legs at 8 m. Never reject an existing save merely because terrain LOD changes.

Parent visual roots to the anchor and calculate their local pose from saved geographic placement. Account for vessel orientation; parenting alone does not keep a deck level if the hab tilts. Rebuild after load, and destroy visuals when packed/unloaded. Do not store raw world position or edit PQS.

### 3.3 Placement from the hab

Open the hab PAW and select Open Lodeworks -> Buildings. The catalog has Included kit, Build more and Upgrade controls. Select a building, display a deck-local ghost at the hovered grid cell, rotate where useful, and click to place. Preview occupied footprint, availability, operating draw, rate/capacity and cost. Invalid placement is red with its actual reason. Escape/cancel changes nothing. Keyboard/controller alternatives may select a cell by dropdown; no raw world-coordinate entry.

Included kit consumes a stored prefab token and installs a complete Tier 0 building immediately. It does not consume Metal/Parts. Build more reserves all listed materials from the local ledger and starts a 30 UT-second construction job using 2 colony power/s; Upgrade uses its incremental recipe and a 60 UT-second job. One automated builder job runs per endpoint; no Kerbal EVA clicking or loaded-scene waiting is required. Pause on no power or invalid anchor. Integrated solar/generator output still follows power simulation. Building jobs join the same chronological simulator and are persisted.

Advance to command UT, validate blueprint, kit/material availability, grid, storage and anchor, then commit atomically. A failed command creates no row, consumes no token/resources and pays no fee. Materials reserved for construction cannot also be shipped or used for maintenance. An unfinished footprint remains occupied but adds no production/storage capability until completion. Its construction materials remain accounted in a job escrow. Cancel before completion restores all reserved materials if capacity permits, otherwise refuse cancellation until space is made; no partial phantom refund.

Move a completed building within the deck for free. Upgrade replaces its stats in the same grid slot, preserving condition, recipe/batch selection, machine identity and fault/repair state; it does not restore condition to 1 for free. Do not upgrade while its repair/transport job is active. New maximum rate/capacity applies at completion; old production machine is stopped during upgrade. Storage retains its old capacity until the upgraded capacity commits; an unfinished upgrade cannot hide already stored goods or release reserved cargo space. Capacity upgrades cannot create inventory or propellant.

### 3.4 Included starter set

The hab includes its built-in basic smelter, owned 20 power/s starter generator, 2000-unit battery and small integrated storage. These require no extra placement. Its separate included kit has exactly one of each:

| Included placeable | Count | Purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| Basic ore drill | 1 | Mine graded ore |
| Fuel-feed pump | 1 | Obtain refinery feed without consuming all campaign ore |
| Basic refinery | 1 | Make stock LiquidFuel/Oxidizer from ore or feed |
| Basic transport terminal | 1 | Handle surface cargo and later fueled launches |
| Ore bin | 1 | Store mined batches |
| Fuel-feed bin | 1 | Buffer raw feed |
| Metal bin | 1 | Store expansion materials |
| LiquidFuel tank | 1 | Store the stock fuel component |
| Oxidizer tank | 1 | Store the stock oxidizer component |

These occupy nine of the available 24 deck cells when all placed. Placement order is the player's choice. A tutorial suggests ore drill -> ore bin -> Metal bin -> refinery/pump/feed bin -> separate fuel tanks -> terminal. A starter terminal may be placed before c01, but its transport services stay locked until the campaign reward. Baseline capabilities must allow production and manufacture of extra basic storage before any advanced blueprint.

Free starter equipment cannot be sold for funds or salvaged into manufactured material. Dismantling a completed starter piece returns its original token, never a new token plus Metal. The piece's condition/fault and identity stay on that token; replacing a worn drill from the same token cannot repair it for free. Upgraded starter pieces return a packed piece at their current tier/state, keeping the upgrade's material investment; they cannot repeatedly refund it while retaining an upgraded prefab. Extra material-built pieces salvage 50% of actually paid Metal/Parts at completed dismantle, never more than the original paid recipe. Show and confirm permanent loss of the remaining material. No refunds for debug-granted equipment.

A hab variant's built-in terminal occupies no deck slot and fulfills the starter terminal entitlement: mark that token installed-in-hab rather than granting a duplicate free terminal. Variants improve their integrated equipment and storage through their explicit profiles, not by receiving multiple starter sets. Initial part prices/masses include their prefabricated kit; record kit mass/cost in the part config and do not mint another paid component on every deployment. Detailed surface-ledger ground-facility mass remains an abstraction, but the player must launch the actual kit-bearing hab.

### 3.5 Expansion and dismantling

Early extra buildings cost Metal only. The first shop costs no Machine Parts because it must be capable of making the first Parts. Advanced buildings/upgrades use both Metal and Parts. Repair continues using Supplies. LiquidFuel/Oxidizer are operating propellants, not a construction currency.

The player can use locally produced materials or optionally import them from another colony. Local production is sufficient for startup and basic expansion; never force importing a construction kit just to begin. Campaign contracts unlock blueprints and remain the main progression quest giver; resource production pays the cost of applying those blueprints.

Do not dismantle a machine being repaired, a terminal with occupied vehicle/handling jobs, or storage whose removal leaves inventory plus reservations above remaining capacity. Salvage itself requires destination storage room before commitment. A building with an unpaid/unfinished construction job follows cancellation rules rather than receiving completed-building salvage. Capacity changes, demolition and salvage occur once at command UT; repeated clicks/reload cannot duplicate materials.

Relocation disables routes, resolves/export/discards inventory with explicit confirmation, and packs remaining starter pieces back into the hab with their saved tier/condition. Other equipment must be salvaged or left behind explicitly; starter-kit entitlements never reset. Ground depletion stays. A new site receives a new colony identity but references the same hab's remaining kit receipt. Orbital depots instead retain their core's built-in starter capabilities; extra modules follow material construction and physical mass accounting.

### 3.6 Colony footprint and expansion

The 24 buildable cells are an intentional v1 limit. No extra deck or merged colony inventory in this release. Upgrade storage/machines in place, dismantle unneeded equipment safely, or launch another hab and connect its separate ledger through normal surface/rocket services. Nearby colonies share geographic deposits where applicable, not storage, crew, power or free transfers. Instantaneous local transfers mean transfers within one endpoint only.

The grid UI shows used/free cells and the footprint of queued jobs. Before starting a build, show the remaining cells and whether the endpoint still has space for the player's selected next facilities; this is advisory, not an invented mandatory layout. Provide a compact starter-layout example that fits all nine tokens plus expansion storage, a shop and power. Packed starter pieces remain recoverable under section 3.4. Never silently expand the deck when storage is full.

## 4 Inventory and provenance

### 4.1 Goods

| Kind | Internal identity | Density t/unit | Uses |
|---|---|---:|---|
| Graded ore | batch ledger entry | 0.010 | Campaign deliveries and smelting |
| Metal | FoundryMetal | 0.010 | Basic construction, storage, upgrades, processing and commercial orders |
| Slag | FoundrySlag | 0.010 | Optional storage/export; dump by default |
| Machine Parts | FoundryParts | 0.005 | Advanced construction/upgrades and commercial/campaign orders |
| Supplies | FoundrySupplies | 0.002 | Locally manufactured maintenance material |
| Fuel feed | internal feed ledger | 0.005 | Pumped raw material for the refinery |
| Liquid Fuel | stock LiquidFuel metadata; ledger inventory | Read verified stock density | Transport fuel and explicit manual stock transfer |
| Oxidizer | stock Oxidizer metadata; ledger inventory | Read verified stock density | Rocket oxidizer and explicit manual stock transfer |

Resource definitions may supply stable metadata and localized names, but no Lodeworks tank consumes these goods in v1. Stock Ore is never consumed or redefined. LiquidFuel and Oxidizer definitions are also never redefined. Read their density and unitCost metadata from the installed game, validate it, and marshal immutable values into the pure sim. Transport mass includes cargo, structure, onboard propellant, and fuel carried as cargo.

Batch fields: batchId, originBody, originBiome, grade, units, provenanceClass, campaignEligible. Mined batches are eligible; debug/tutorial grants are ineligible. Origin never becomes the receiving body's identity. Smelting consumes ore and does not produce eligible ore again.

Stack only entries matching originBody, originBiome, exact campaign grade band, and provenance eligibility. Grade bands: poor [0,0.40), normal [0.40,0.70), rich [0.70,1]. Store weighted average grade. Campaign ore filters use these bands, not arbitrary minimum-grade thresholds; therefore averaging within a band cannot turn poor ore into rich qualifying cargo. If later adding exact-grade objectives, preserve sublots before enabling those objectives.

Default processing selects oldest eligible stored batch by creation sequence, then batchId. UI allows richest first, poorest first, or a pinned batch/band. A policy is persisted. Switching policy never changes batch grade or inventory.

### 4.2 Resource-specific storage

Every storage building has exactly one selected resource kind, locked when built. An ore bin holds all graded ore batches against one combined ore cap; grades/origins still stay separate in inventory. A LiquidFuel tank cannot hold Oxidizer; Parts bins cannot hold Metal. No universal silo automatically grants every resource 4000 capacity. Use distinct catalog names/colors/icons even if primitive art is reused.

| Resource | Hab integrated capacity | Basic dedicated unit added capacity | Advanced unit added capacity |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Graded ore | 100 total ore units | 400 | 1600 |
| Metal | 100 | 400 | 1600 |
| Slag | 0 | 400 | 1600 |
| Machine Parts | 20 | 200 | 800 |
| Supplies | 20 | 200 | 800 |
| Fuel feed | 100 | 400 | 1600 |
| LiquidFuel | 45 | 2000 | 8000 |
| Oxidizer | 55 | 2000 | 8000 |

The basic hab holds only 0.5 t usable stock-density propellant in its integrated buffers. Its included separate tanks must be placed for a larger fuel reserve. Empty starter/storage buildings do not grant content. Show integrated and building-added capacity separately. Storage can be duplicated within deck slots or upgraded in the same slot. Do not allow resource switching while a bin is occupied, reserved or referenced by jobs/routes; v1 requires dismantle/rebuild for a different resource type.

Basic storage recipes are baseline blueprints. Advanced non-fuel storage follows c05; advanced LF/Oxidizer tanks follow d01 and still require locally made or delivered Parts. Parts/Supplies bins may be built before the shop unlock, but have no output until the recipe unlock. Advanced storage is the c05 reward and costs Parts. A finished bin changes only its own resource cap. Filled removed storage must be emptied first, not silently dumped into hidden hab storage. Depot-specific dedicated modules follow section 9.5; core-built capacities are limited but real, and expansion is per resource as well.

Capacities are additive only for matching resource kinds. Full non-dump outputs stop their entire recipe. Slag dump tracks discarded totals but creates no credits or campaign progress.

### 4.3 Transactions

All amounts must be finite and nonnegative. Reject NaN/infinity and invalid IDs at input boundaries. Clamp floating residue only within epsilon 1e-9; do not conceal meaningful shortages.

Career funds are owned by stock Funding, not copied into an independent colony currency. The adapter supplies a validated funds budget and commits actual charges/refunds through verified stock APIs on the main thread. Every funds operation has a saved receipt and is reconciled after reload; never replay a charge merely because a cosmetic model respawned. During catch-up, do not spend the same available funds concurrently through two routes. Pause a departure if its funds transaction cannot be committed, and preserve the remaining simulation cursor.

Use commandId to reject duplicate commands within the saved command ledger. Booking validates a real located vehicle, route/return safety, cargo/fuel/space/berth/parking claims and affordability before creating a committed job. Each timed transfer atomically debits its sole source owner, credits its sole receiving owner, consumes applicable power, and writes a receipt. First actual handling/pumping work commits the quoted handling fee through the stock funds boundary; a zero-work rocket leg (already fueled, empty cargo) commits its base fee atomically at departure authorization instead. Electric service prepays its cycle at first loading under 9.3, with no proportional second debit. If a fee cannot commit, neither matter nor power advances. Departure revalidates safety, changes the fully loaded vehicle to transit, creates its flight record and releases obsolete source claims; it removes no loaded matter again. Burn events debit the vehicle's single propulsion balance later. Failed transitions change nothing.

#### 4.3.1 Sole-ownership shipment transition table

Each row is a persisted state, not an animation. Track partial quantities and receipts per consignment/leg/job; the table applies to cargo fuel as cargo as well as ore/products/hulls. An onward handoff starts another leg with the same consignment/history, not a new eligible original dispatch.

| State / transition | Sole matter owner and physical mass | Space and claims | Debit, cancellation, destruction and receipt |
|---|---|---|---|
| Source inventory | Source ledger; station mass includes stored matter | Occupies source storage; no destination guarantee before booking | No handling debit. Source destruction loses only matter actually there; no delivery credit |
| Committed reservation | Still source ledger, never a second owner; claims have no mass | Exact lots/fuel unavailable to others; destination incoming space plus berth/disposition and payload claimed | Booking receipt, no burn/handling debit. Cancel releases claims once. Source loss loses untransferred stock |
| Loading escrow/onboard, partially timed | Transferred cargo belongs to vehicle loading manifest; remainder stays in source. Propulsion pumped so far belongs only to vehicle tanks | Each transferred unit frees source storage but holds equal source return-capacity until departure/cancel; destination reservation stays. Manifest and propulsion have separate claims/tank limits | Rocket: 0.5 power/cargo unit actually loaded; pump separately. Electric: full cycle prepaid at first loading, no per-unit second charge. Fee once at first work, no refund. Cancel returns surviving unburned matter using return capacity, or waits for valid storage; no teleport if source gone. Quantity/energy receipts persist |
| Waiting launch window | Fully loaded vehicle owns cargo and its distinct propulsion; counted at the physical endpoint, not also in source | Destination guarantee and source return-capacity retained; berth released only after safe reserved parking move | No burn, idle handling draw or extra fee. Cancel before departure returns unburned balances once. Destroyed vehicle loses onboard matter; endpoint loss suspends/replans, never retrospectively destroys a surviving vehicle |
| Departure / transit | Vehicle manifest owns cargo; vehicle tanks own propulsion; no endpoint mass in transit | Release source return-capacity/unused matter claims; retain destination capacity/berth, safe return/onward guarantees and home parking entitlement | Atomic departure receipt locks first-dispatch eligibility. Scheduled burns each debit actual propulsion once. No postdeparture cancellation refund; a new feasible divert/return is a paid plan. Source loss cannot erase transit matter |
| Arriving / partial unloading | Unloaded portions belong to destination ledger or final consumer; remaining portions only to vehicle | Convert incoming reservation proportionally to stored space; retain claims for remainder. Count berthed onboard matter once in station mass | Rocket: 0.5 power/cargo unit actually unloaded; electric unloading is included in prepaid cycle. No second handling funds fee. Outage/space failure retains bounded onboard remainder. Destination loss affects stored portions; surviving cargo follows recovery rules. Partial delivery receipts prevent replay |
| Destination inventory / final consumption | Intermediate ledger owns stored cargo; KSC/objective receiver consumes only authorized final quantities | Release completed cargo/berth claims, retain vehicle's valid parking or next-leg booking | One final claim per quantity and objective under 11.1; canceled/no-objective cargo follows 9.2. Destruction never awards receipt. Onward handoff preserves provenance, eligibility and already claimed quantities |

Loading escrow is physically on the located abstract vehicle, not extra stock hidden on the terminal. At a depot it contributes core vessel mass while berthed/parked; ground-facility mass remains the stated surface abstraction. Source return-capacity is a capacity reservation, not duplicate material; cancellation atomically releases its own proportional capacity claim while returning that matter, never another job's promise. Destruction releases affected claims once and records loss by current owner; verify actual vehicle loss rather than equating a vessel GUID remap with destruction. Cancellation may return only surviving, unburned balances and cannot refund spent handling energy or fees. Unloading cannot be canceled back into already consumed KSC cargo.

Refueling is a separate timed transfer from local ledger/cargo to the one mutable vehicle propulsion owner; moving fuel cargo into propulsion reduces the manifest and any deliverable claim by that exact amount. Flight/shipment records reference tanks; they never persist a second mutable fuel balance. Power/partial-work receipts survive interruption/reload, so resume charges only remaining units. Acceptance in phases 12/13: partial load/cancel/full source, reload at every row, source/vehicle/destination loss at each ownership boundary, no cargo both stored/onboard, mass before/after handoff, burn-before-divert with no refundable burned fuel, fee once and partial unload once. Phase 2A supplies escrow primitives, not this future transport state machine.

Reservations reduce usable destination space for both arrivals and production. Released reservations cannot create negative reserved capacity. Storage upgrades/dismantling validate this invariant. KSC is a consuming endpoint and does not reserve warehouse capacity.

### 4.4 Unified reservations and allocation

Implement ReservationBook in Lodeworks.Sim as the sole authority for promises against inventory, tank space, vehicle capacity and handling resources. All constructors, routes, maintenance, refueling and manual transfers call it; no subsystem keeps a competing mutable reserved-total dictionary. Derived UI totals are read-only. Each commitment has reservationId, ownerId/type, endpointId, resource or slot, quantity, eligible lot references where required, createdUT, priority, state and release/commit receipt. Reservation entries are persisted with schema 6.

| Concept | Effect |
|---|---|
| Protected stock target | Persisted policy floor used by automatic consumers/exporters; does not subtract matter or reserve specific lots |
| Committed matter reservation | Claims existing specific eligible inventory for one job; unavailable to every other consumer |
| Incoming capacity reservation | Claims destination capacity without adding inventory or physical mass |
| Forecast requirement | Read-only expected future need; never treated as guaranteed fuel or stock |

For a consumer, usable inventory equals physically available, unescrowed matter minus committed claims and applicable protected floors; clamp only numerical residue. Multiple floors protecting the same stock use the maximum applicable floor, not their sum. Campaign ore targets and player floors protect matching eligibility/origin/band sets; keep overlapping sets explicit so broad and narrow filters cannot double-count protection. A quota protection is bounded by the remaining eligible objective amount minus eligible assigned consignments already committed/in transit. It is not a promise that ore will exist. Quota-authorized export may consume its own protected quantity; unrelated export/refining cannot. Processing policies select only uncommitted lots.

Escrow physically owns material removed from inventory; its reservation refers to that escrow and must not subtract the same units again. Transition existing stock -> reserved -> escrow/onboard -> consumed/delivered exactly once. Future fuel production and en-route fuel cargo are forecasts until material arrives and is committed; auto-dispatch requiring destination fuel must have actual guarantees under section 9.7.

Existing committed jobs win over new requests; priorities never steal a live reservation. Simultaneous new automatic requests sort by priority, request-createdUT, then stable ID. Assign higher shipped-default priority to maintenance and operational refueling than discretionary exports; construction uses explicit player commands. At equal UT player commands follow section 12's ordering. A manual build requiring 60 Metal from 100 commits it; a later route asking for 80 sees only 40, and either waits or uses an allowed partial manifest. Reversing the chronological requests can reverse the result, deterministically.

Manual stock transfers and override commands show affected protected floors. Overriding a floor requires an explicit command; canceling committed reservations requires showing all affected jobs/plans and applying each job's cancellation rules before revalidation. Never silently release propulsion or return guarantees. Cancellation/owner destruction releases claims once; rebuilding reservation indexes on load validates quantities and quarantines conflicts instead of duplicating stock. Show owner, quantity and reason for unavailable inventory.

Implementation: first build pure Reserve/Commit/Release/Query operations and conservation fixtures; then migrate callers to these APIs; finally integrate chronological competition. Tests include construction vs export, refinery vs quota, overlapping floors, two tugs vs stock transfer, escrow accounting, partial release, duplicate release, and reload with active reservations.

## 5 Mining and deposits

### 5.1 Separate extraction

Drills extract into stored ore batches even if every smelter is off. Smelters process stored batches even after the source vein is exhausted. Never apply vein fullness to an already mined batch's processing rate.

Cell size is 0.5 degrees. Normalize longitude to [-180,180), clamp latitude to [-90,90], and define the +90 pole index as the final valid latitude cell. Store cellId = bodyName|latIndex|lonIndex. All colonies in the same cell share its remaining ore and initial grade. Display this before deployment so two nearby habs cannot appear to own independent deposits.

Use stable FNV-1a hashing of UTF-8 canonical values, not string.GetHashCode or Unity randomness. Initial grade = 0.25 + 0.70 * hashUnit(bodyName, latIndex, lonIndex, "grade"). Initial capacity = 20000 * veinScale * clamp(stockOreAbundance,0.05,1). Query stock abundance through a verified read-only API; if unavailable, use 0.5 and show that the deposit is estimated. Persist capacity/grade on first survey; later changes to profiles cannot refill an old deposit.

Provide a read-only prospecting panel for the current site and discovered colony sites. It shows grade band, estimated capacity, hardness, power demand, and shared cell identity. No scanning minigame and no automatic reveal of every planetary deposit.

### 5.2 Declining extraction

Basic drill nominal rate = 0.50 ore units/s before crew. Base draw = 6 colony power units/s multiplied by hardness and crew. A running drill's actual rate falls with remaining/capacity. Zero power, full storage, no crew, or invalid site stops it.

Use the analytic solution when fill varies: with nominal rate q and cell capacity C, remaining(t) = remaining(0) * exp(-q*t/C); extracted = remaining(0)-remaining(t). Power use scales with the same fill, giving 12*hardness power units per extracted ore unit. For multiple active drills in one cell, sum q, then allocate extraction proportionally over the same interval; never let iteration order let each drill see a fresh deposit. Split at storage or power exhaustion events.

At remaining <= max(1e-6, C*1e-6), explicitly extract the affordable residual and mark exhausted. Handle insufficient power/storage by leaving residual until affordable. This prevents an asymptotic deposit from claiming to be empty while never finishing.

