## 8 Maintenance and faults

Each drill, smelter, fuel-feed pump, refinery, shop, and continuous generator has condition [0,1]. Passive storage/batteries, terminals and transport hulls do not have condition wear in v1; do not invent an additional fleet maintenance system. Wear applies only to actual operating seconds; base wear 0.000002/s, configurable per building. Stop exactly at condition zero, including during long catch-up; do not apply a long productive step and discover afterward that it should have failed halfway through.

Default mode supports automatic preventive maintenance once its service blueprint is unlocked at c05. Before c05, manual Supply-based service and the emergency Service Visit are available; an enabled AutoMaintenance preference does not bypass the blueprint gate. At condition <=0.35, a building with an Engineer, 20 local Supplies, and a functioning maintenance service begins a 60 UT-second service. Reserve Supplies once at start; the building pauses during service. Service proceeds loaded or unloaded and across warp, consumes its reservation at completion, clears faults, and restores condition to 1. Persist service state and remaining duration. If no supplies exist, continue until a fault/zero condition stops it and show the shortage. Disabling automatic maintenance permits manual start of the same job.

The hab has an emergency Service Visit option costing 500 funds in career, or zero funds in other modes, with a 60 UT-second service time. It requires an Engineer but no Supplies. This prevents a first-colony deadlock where the shop needed to produce Supplies is itself broken. It services only the selected building and never adds inventory. Repeated jobs must charge once each. Supply-based maintenance remains preferable for established networks.

Optional Hard Maintenance mode makes jobs advance only while loaded at warp <=4x. Persist pause state and show why. Default campaign does not require repeatedly visiting colonies to wait through repair timers. Crew departure pauses service; reservation remains. Cancel returns reserved Supplies, refunds no charged service-visit funds, and resets job state.

Fault occurrence is deterministic: once per new 3600 s UT window for machines that actually operated during the preceding window, threshold = max(0,0.50-condition). Use hash(buildingId,window,"occurrence") for occurrence. Use a separate hash(buildingId,window,"type") for jam/wear/short with equal thirds. Consumer jam stops; wear multiplies effective rates by 0.5; short triples power demand only. Faults do not stack. All three types must be reachable. Persist last evaluated window; machines off for years must not roll millions of idle faults.

Continuous generators instead map jam to zero output (mechanical stop), wear to 0.5 rated output, and short to zero output with protective electrical isolation and zero parasitic demand. Never triple a nonexistent generator demand or turn output into negative generation. Generator operation is crew-independent; condition-zero, jam, short or service stops output and operating-time wear. Normal/wear generation accrues wear only while enabled and producing, including full-battery discarded power; halted/off/service time accrues none. No fleet wear is added. Manual Supplies service and the 500-funds/zero-other-mode emergency visit can repair an isolated generator with an Engineer even when battery is empty: the service timer itself requires no electrical power. Phase 9 must test all mappings, half-output integration, wear-zero boundary, empty-power recovery, crew departure/service pause and replay/round-trip fault windows.

Difficulty options: wear multiplier, faults enabled, auto maintenance enabled, hard maintenance. No crew death or stock part failure system.

### 8.1 Maintenance pacing acceptance

At wear multiplier 1, uninterrupted operation reaches condition zero after 500000 s (about 23.15 Kerbin days); preventive threshold 0.35 occurs after 325000 operating seconds. These are initial game-balance values, not verified pacing. Wear uses actual operating time, so outages/services do not advance it. c05 still gates automatic preventive maintenance; before that, manual Supply service and the paid emergency visit remain available.

The starter guidance recommends an Engineer and shows repair eligibility. A crew-only colony may operate at its stated efficiency, but cannot be presented as repair-self-sufficient. Engineer absence pauses repair with an actionable message; never generate an Engineer or convert API failure into crew loss.

Calibrate c01-c05 using feasible flight windows and the starter production rates. Record service visits per campaign stage, forced colony switches, productive time lost and Supplies demand. Default service proceeds in background; no colony visit is required merely to watch a timer. Target zero mandatory scene visits solely for service timers and no mandatory emergency repair before the near-KSC c02 reference completion. Persistent early repair repetition requires an explicit balance amendment, not undocumented adapter changes.

## 9 Fuel production and transport infrastructure

### 9.1 Propellant resources and factories

Use LiquidFuel plus Oxidizer for every rocket template in this release. Match the stock 0.9:1.1 unit consumption ratio; with stock equal densities this is 45% LiquidFuel and 55% Oxidizer by mass. Validate densities at startup and compute mixture mass from both; never assume one resource's units equal tonnes. Do not add hydrogen, methane, boil-off, or nuclear-only fuel modes yet. This is KSP-consistent fuel accounting, not chemical modeling of actual regolith.

The basic refinery and fuel-feed pump are baseline buildings, available before the first off-world shipment. The first of each is included in the starter kit; extras are built from local Metal. Fuel is never granted by placing a building. A new endpoint must actually produce, receive, or explicitly buy local fuel before its transports can depart.

The refinery has two mutually exclusive modes:

- Ore ISRU consumes 0.25 units/s of Lodeworks graded ore plus 15 colony power/s. At default stock densities, produces 0.18 LiquidFuel/s, 0.22 Oxidizer/s, and 0.05 Slag/s. 80% of input mass becomes mixture; 20% becomes slag. Grade affects Metal smelting, not fuel yield. Consumed ore loses campaign eligibility and cannot be counted as returned ore. Origin still appears in production diagnostics.
- Fuel-feed mode consumes 0.50 feed units/s plus 10 power/s. At default densities, produces the same fuel/oxidizer/slag outputs as above. No direct creation of fuel from electricity alone.

Generalize rates from target mixture mass and runtime densities so changed metadata cannot create mass. Both outputs must have storage room; unlike Slag, neither fuel nor oxidizer is dumped by default. Production stalls if either would overflow. Crew, wear, repair, priority and background integration apply to the pump and refinery. Implement the recipe through the same resource math as the other factories.

Pump output is 0.50 feed units/s at full reservoir and crew factor 1, using 6 power/s before crew. It taps an independent finite fuel-feed reservoir in the same geographic cell, not the metal-ore vein. Reservoir capacity = 20000 * volatileFactor units. Factors: Kerbin 2.0, Mun 0.5, Minmus 2.0, Duna 1.5, Ike 0.5, Dres 0.5, Laythe 2.0, Vall 1.5, Tylo 0.25, Bop 0.5, Pol 1.0, Eeloo 2.0, Eve 1.0, Gilly 0.25, unknown 0.5. These are configurable game abstractions; label the deposit Fuel feed, not a claim that raw finished rocket fuel lies underground. Apply the same analytic declining extraction/residual rule as ore, with no stock abundance query required. Multiple pumps share reservoir depletion.

Base colony integrated and dedicated storage follows section 4.2. LiquidFuel and Oxidizer require separate tank placeables; building one does not add capacity to the other or to feed/ore/products. Display available mixture, individual components, and unusable imbalance. Allow fuel as cargo, either a paired mixture manifest or individual component manifest; paired shipments use the 0.9:1.1 ratio. Carrying fuel as cargo consumes payload mass in addition to propulsion fuel.

Keep a refinery's reserve policy: do not consume ore earmarked for accepted export quotas or below a player-set ore reserve. Player can explicitly override this. No hidden infinite fuel reservoir or automatic remote purchase.

### 9.2 Endpoints and vehicle location

Endpoint kinds: surface colony, registered orbital depot, and ksc-receiver. Same-body surface service is a configurable electrically powered ground/drone abstraction and consumes power; any surface-to-orbit, orbit-to-surface, or cross-body route requires rocket propellant. Do not allow a ground drone to cross bodies or reach a station.

Surface colonies require an enabled terminal. Depots require enabled cargo handling and berths. KSC has both inherently. KSC consumes only quantities assigned to accepted qualifying objectives, or explicitly authorized fuel sales/continuous-export disposal. Unmatched ore/products/excess are not automatically rewarded or destroyed: booking must include a confirmed no-reward disposal authorization, otherwise refuse that manifest. Exceptional legacy/mismatched arrivals remain onboard in visible waiting-for-disposition, not in an invented KSC warehouse, until a valid onward plan or confirmed disposal. Continuous ore/product export is explicit irreversible disposal with zero reward and a quote warning, not an automatic sale. Fuel cargo can be explicitly sold at stock metadata price after the leg's handling fee, with a preview, and earns no ore objective. Unsold fuel stays onboard. A transport physically at KSC may buy its own next-leg fuel using actual funds; remote fueling requires local inventory or delivered fuel. Section 17.1 defines receipts, prices/refunds and mode behavior.

Replace the old return-time-only slot with a real scenario-owned TransportVehicle record: id, templateId, homeEndpointId, currentEndpointId or transitId, cargo capacity, tank capacity, onboard LiquidFuel/Oxidizer, state, assigned route, paid hull cost, and availability UT. Cosmetic appearance does not define vehicle capabilities. A vehicle arrives physically in the ledger at its destination; it cannot simultaneously serve its origin or teleport home.

Loading and unloading each consume 0.5 owned power per cargo unit for rocket services; electric service uses prepaid full-cycle energy instead. Handling fee is 25 + 10*bookedCargoMassT per rocket leg, or 25 + 0.2*bookedCargoUnits per electric cycle, once at first handling/pumping work (zero-work rocket leg at departure authorization). The fee covers loading/unloading, not another arrival fee. Freeze the manifest before debit; extra cargo requires a new quoted job. KSC supplies local power; other endpoints use owned power. Surface throughput is 1 t/min, upgraded 4 t/min, one loader; pumping/concurrency follows 7.1.2. Timed ownership/cancellation follows 4.3.1. Spent energy/fees are not refundable.

Vehicles are commissioned through a terminal/depot hangar. Surface commissioning costs the template hull funds, represents deliberate local fabrication/service anywhere on a valid surface (including off-world), requires no imported building materials, and takes 600 powered UT seconds at 2 owned power/s to add an empty vehicle. This is an explicit surface-service abstraction, not remote orbital hull creation. Orbital commissioning instead orders a dry hull from a selected surface terminal; ship that hull as transportHull cargo with template dry mass, paying actual ascent/transfer fuel and handling. Delivered hull plus 600 powered seconds of setup becomes a located vehicle without changing mass or charging another hull fee. The hull order is paid once and retains its ID; never mint propellant or convert one hull into two vehicles. Cancellation before setup completion returns the surviving dry hull to local storage/escrow; completed surface fabrication cost is not refunded. Retirement of reusable vehicles returns remaining unburned fuel to available local capacity and refunds 50% of actual stock hull funds paid once; refuse until storage fits or explicit discard is confirmed. Destruction has no refund. Section 17.1 defines free-mode and expendable-capsule exceptions. Depot berth/hangar limits bound the fleet.

Awaiting-setup dry hulls reserve/occupy one existing hangar/parking entitlement each, including after canceled setup; no unlimited hull warehouse or new resource bin. Delivery books that entitlement before loading, preserves its physical hull mass once and converts the same entitlement to vehicle parking at completion. If no slot exists, block delivery/order; an explicitly booked onward hull shipment can release it only as the hull physically leaves. Destruction loses that owner's hull, never fabricates a vehicle/refund. Retirement requires an empty cargo manifest and resolved shipment/setup/refuel/berth/return commitments through existing cancellation/recovery rules; refuse while any live job still relies on the vehicle.

Player route policy selects one-way relocation or a repeatable cycle with actual outbound/return edges. A repeatable vehicle is available at its home only after the return flight arrives and local turnaround is complete. Empty return flights require their own fuel budget. Fuel can be reserved at the destination before departure, or carried on board for return, subject to tank/mass limits. If neither is possible, block a repeat-cycle departure. A one-way relocation may proceed without a return plan, but show its new location and remaining fuel.

