- c08 binds a valid Duna depot and a surface supplier. After acceptance, two distinct cycles each unload at least 1 t newly surface-produced fuel cargo into that depot and return their supply vehicle with actual burns and fees. Second dispatch follows first turnaround. Fuel stays real usable ledger fuel; tag credited sublots/consignments so its recirculation cannot count as a second new supply cycle. No requirement for the still-locked c08 fleet expansion.
- c09 retains at least 150 total Dres ore to KSC. A throughput facet additionally records at least 50 qualifying units within one 216000-second (10 Kerbin day) window. Start at the first eligible KSC receipt, not acceptance/departure; the inclusive end timestamp qualifies. If below threshold on expiry, the next qualifying receipt starts a new window. Persist startUT, receipt IDs and window units; retain total progress. A receipt may update the total and its same-quest throughput facet, but has only one cargo claim/reward owner. If 150 total is reached without the facet, explicitly authorize additional Dres delivery quota for the remaining window demonstration (up to 50 in a fresh window), consuming that cargo once; do not stop all routes at 150 and softlock the facet. No deadline/failure penalty or inventory polling. Warn about remaining required ore; another site/reoffer is permitted.

Durable counters record actual receipt quantities, completed-cycle IDs, first/second dispatch and return timestamps, automation flag, paid service/burn receipts and production identities. Never poll inventory for completion. Cancel/reoffer retains completed demonstrations and total progress; expired windows remain diagnostics, not lost permanent progress. Phase 15 defines evaluators and Phase 16 config/graph fixtures; phases 13/14 supply real handoff/cycle records.

Quantities must be checked against low and typical deposit capacities, including ore consumed to fuel the network. Main c03 permits a direct early shuttle delivery if feasible; d01/d02 teach and then require a functioning depot network before c06. Do not require a still-locked capsule in d02: c03 unlocks it first. A d01 fuel delivery must contain fuel as cargo unloaded into the depot, not the vehicle onboard propellant already counted in its burns. d02 binds named depot IDs at acceptance and uses the consignment handoff history; touching a depot icon or rewriting an itinerary cannot satisfy it. A commercial fuel sale must consume fuel at the named endpoint and cannot repeatedly circulate the same mixture for reward.

Quantities must be checked against low/typical/high deposit capacities under 17.2. These are initial balance values, not proof of one-site feasibility. Multiple colonies and optional material imports are permitted; show expected amounts and depletion/multi-site estimates. Main quests have no deadline; capability windows can be retried without failing the quest.

Baseline capabilities exist before c01: basic hab, its smelter/generator/battery, exactly one complete included starter set, basic dedicated-storage recipes, and manual emergency service. First shop uses Metal only. All production needed to pay for extra basic buildings is available without Machine Parts. c01 unlocks the surface service needed for c02. Early rockets/depots/tugs unlock together at c02 so the player can bootstrap a genuine fueled network without needing its own later output. Ordinary stock research prerequisites may delay access to the basic hab but must not require a Lodeworks quest that needs that hab. No blueprint requires its own output or capability to obtain itself.

### 11.3 Parts and unlock enforcement

Campaign parts are original Lodeworks hab variants using referenced stock model paths until original art exists. All may establish a basic colony; variants improve built-in equipment without requiring a new resource catalog:

- lw-hab-250: baseline; 2000 battery, 20 generation, base capacities.
- lw-hab-remote: 4000 battery and integrated basic transport terminal, but no free vehicles or fuel.
- lw-hab-heavy: 8000 battery, twice the baseline per-resource integrated buffer capacities, and two integrated vehicle hangar capacities, but no free vehicles or fuel. It receives one normal starter set, not multiple.
- lw-hab-survey: remote capabilities plus richer on-site prospecting display; it does not reveal unvisited deposits globally.
- lw-hab-endurance: heavy capabilities plus 40 continuous generation and built-in preventive service.

Use one explicit blueprint registry mapping campaign rewards to part internal names, building kinds, upgrades, and service capabilities. No arbitrary stock engine or tank is locked or patched. Future rewards can add original parts through configuration.

Before implementing the full campaign, prove a public-API adapter can keep unrewarded Lodeworks parts unavailable in the editor/R&D while enabling them per save after reward. Verify AvailablePart, ResearchAndDevelopment, purchase/tech state, editor refresh, and stock save behavior against installed assemblies. Do not assume setting a UI flag alone enforces a lock. The adapter must prevent ordinary entry-cost purchase from bypassing campaign requirements, and respect any stock science prerequisite. Granting the blueprint permanently satisfies the campaign requirement; no duplicate entry payment is required for the awarded Lodeworks part, but unmet stock tech still needs research.

Prototype tests: locked part cannot be selected/bought; reward enables it; switching to another save restores that save's locks; sandbox has all parts; existing vessels with locked parts still load; an imported saved craft containing a locked part cannot be launched as a bypass in career (use a verified editor/launch validation API). Do not alter shared part prefab state or other saves. If public APIs cannot meet these conditions without Harmony, mark this feasibility phase blocked with observed signatures and the failing behavior. Do not replace actual part unlocks with a cosmetic badge and claim completion.

### 11.4 Commercial contracts

After c05, offer optional Metal and Parts orders delivered to KSC, and supply deliveries to named existing colonies. Reward funds and reputation, not mandatory progression blueprints. Select reachable existing endpoints and available service tiers. Deadlines must be at least known earliest round-trip/production estimate plus 10 Kerbin days; show assumptions. Never offer Sun/Jool surface targets. Cancel/fail cannot undo campaign blueprints or delete unrelated shipments. Failure funds = reward/5 and reputation penalty 2, applied once through verified stock contract mechanisms.

Section 17.1 defines configured commercial reward values and one-time failure receipts. Accepted named-colony orders consume only explicitly assigned objective cargo; ordinary unassigned deliveries store goods. No reward merely for circulating fuel/ore. Optional commercial contracts in science/sandbox use the campaign window with zero funds/reputation, the same physical delivery rules and optional deadlines; never debit a nonexistent Funding account.

## 12 Simulation ordering and time

WorldSimulator.Advance(targetUT) is the sole clock owner. It advances colony extraction, processing, power, maintenance, shipments, vehicle arrivals/turnaround, routes, fuel manufacture, vehicle loading/burns, refuels, depot handoffs, orbital environment updates and campaign events in chronological order. Loaded, packed, unloaded, space-center, and tracking-station views use the same function. Update displayed state before saving or evaluating a contract at its timestamp. No factories tick in the editor; the editor may read campaign unlocks only.

Use event-based integration with shared intervals. Interval boundaries include construction/upgrade completion, launch windows, burn events, refuel/handling completion, vehicle ready times, shipment arrivals, slot ready times, service completion, wear zero, fault windows, solar samples, route scheduling boundaries, batch exhaustion, deposit threshold, storage full/empty, and power full/empty. Inputs changing during a gap must affect the remaining gap. A cargo arrival at t=100 can feed a shop between t=100 and targetUT; it cannot arrive only after all production has finished.

At equal timestamps use this order: integrate the previous open interval; commit completions and arrivals; apply maintenance/fault transitions; apply external snapshot/journal replacements; apply player commands; evaluate route departures; evaluate campaign completion/rewards. External replacements precede new commands/departures so destroyed/invalid anchors cannot dispatch, but do not rewrite the interval or completions just ended. Independent simulation entities sort by stable ID. External events sort by persisted capture sequence then stable eventId; never sort a causal dock/undock or crew replacement sequence lexically by vessel GUID. Inventory/power sharing is integrated over the same interval with deterministic priorities; RecipeMath alone is not a world scheduler.

Tests must prove partition invariance for shared power, drill-to-smelter-to-shop chains, a full output later drained by another building, mid-gap shipments, fuel burn/refuel events, window opening, depot handoffs, faults, maintenance, and routes. Compare Advance(T) against arbitrary partitions to T. Commands/environment snapshots occur at the same absolute timestamps in both tests. Allow absolute 1e-7 units plus relative 1e-9 for long runs. Determinism means these defined rules, not exact real KSP physics.

Catch-up complexity is O(events and fixed environment samples), not O(elapsed seconds). No claim of O(1) when processing fault/solar/route boundaries. Skip idle spans analytically. Set a per-frame processing budget (default 4 ms), persist a targetUT cursor, show Catching up, and continue on later frames. Never discard unprocessed elapsed time or advance lastSimUT past it. Disable mutating UI commands for entities not yet caught up. Save intermediate state with its actual simulation cursor; resume after reload. Do not evaluate completion/rewards against stale world time.

Use UT, not DateTime or Unity delta time, for all gameplay totals. If UT rolls back, restore the loaded save's state and cursor; do not retain newer singleton state. Corrupt negative clock gaps reset the affected cursor with diagnostic. Excessive future gaps are processed incrementally rather than pretending skipped time was simulated. Settings may cap accepted corrupt targets, but the warning and unapplied interval must remain explicit.

### 12.1 External KSP capture during backlog

Blocking mod UI cannot block stock crew transfers, docking/undocking, recovery, destruction or orbit maneuvers. The main-thread adapter must capture immutable timestamped external events even while WorldSimulator's cursor trails live UT; callbacks enqueue, never mutate historical sim state directly. Journal fields: stable eventId, captureSequence (monotonic per save), effectiveUT, observedUT, timestampQuality (`exact`/`observed-bounded`/`unknown`), uncertaintyStartUT, event kind, affected stable anchor/entity IDs, priorSnapshotId, replacementSnapshotId and immutable verified crew/location/orbit/validity payload. Vessel GUIDs remain references. Duplicate ID/payload is no-op; conflicting reuse quarantines and diagnoses.

Apply interval [cursor,eventUT) with the pre-event verified snapshot, then replace only affected fields at eventUT under section 12 tie order. Multiple equal-UT replacements retain capture order. New observations extend backlog target without skipping the cursor. Persist pending journal, applied receipts, capture sequence, target/cursor, current and previous verified snapshots needed by unprocessed intervals. Release prior snapshots only after no pending interval/event refers to them. Quickload/save switch replaces the journal, snapshots, sequence, receipts and all adapter caches atomically; it never merges newer observations from the abandoned timeline.

Persist the completed equal-UT phase/sequence frontier as well as cursor. Capture queued events before processing that boundary. A newly observed event cannot be inserted into an already completed earlier phase at the same UT; reject/quarantine late insertion with a reconciliation diagnostic, or use a genuinely later observed timestamp under the declared approximation policy. Never sort a newly inserted external replacement ahead of commands/burns already committed, silently undo stock funds, or assume cursorUT alone describes an unfinished equal-time boundary.

Verify actual target-assembly callback signatures and their timing/payload for crew, docking/identity, recovery/destruction and orbit changes in Phase 4 (and Phase 11 orbit integration); do not infer them from API names or claim they have been verified here. Existing Phase 1 evidence verifies only its recorded probes/observations. Combine available callbacks with explicit state observation at driver updates/scene transitions as required; absence of a maneuver callback is not permission to backdate current geometry.

When no exact UT is available, stamp observedUT and declare the uncertainty interval since the last verified observation. The maximum accepted approximation is configurable, initially 1 UT second, only if measured observation cadence proves that bound; it is not an assumed callback guarantee during warp/unloaded gaps. If the bound is exceeded or ownership/safety/funds outcomes could differ even within it, suspend affected historical production/dispatch/claims from uncertaintyStartUT, preserve all matter and cursor, and show a diagnostic requiring verified recovery. If already advanced past that boundary, quarantine affected results for explicit reconciliation against saved checkpoints; do not silently replay stock charges or retroactively apply new crew/orbit. Keep the authoritative world cursor pinned at the unresolved boundary rather than independently skipping endpoints. Conservative suspension is not a death/loss claim.

Provide an explicit `Reconcile external observation` recovery command available while ordinary mutating UI is blocked. It operates only on the named blocked journal item, with command/decision receipt and a preview of affected intervals/entities/claims, not arbitrary building/route edits. If verified historical evidence supplies the exact transition, validate it and resume journal replay from a compatible checkpoint; if already committed later stock transactions would conflict, require loading the corresponding complete KSP save rather than rewinding only the mod ledger or replaying charges.

When exact history is unavailable and cursor has not passed uncertaintyStartUT, offer confirmed conservative recovery: record [uncertaintyStartUT,observedUT) as **suspended, not productively simulated**, with zero affected production/extraction/generation/handling/service/wear progress and no new affected dispatch/campaign claims. Preserve matter, prior receipts and reservations; do not pay back service energy, mint refunds or skip known irreversible burns. Process known transit burns once and hold uncertain arrivals as vehicle-owned cargo awaiting safe disposition; shift handling bookings. Unaffected known events retain chronological order. At observedUT adopt the verified replacement snapshot, revalidate held jobs/plans, close the journal blocker once and resume normal advancement. This explicitly recorded conservative lost-opportunity interval is not a normal partition-invariant productive run or a claim to have reconstructed stock history.

If cursor already passed the uncertain boundary, do not approve conservative recovery over previously credited/consumed outputs: offer the verified complete-save rollback path and preserve a diagnostic/export of the discrepancy. The safe recovery path always remains available from a compatible complete save/checkpoint; never require fabrication of an exact event UT. Phase 4 must test recovery-command availability under backlog, confirmed suspended-interval replay with known burns/held arrivals, receipts across save/load and complete-save rollback replacing both journal and stock state. Show the precise required save when automatic correction is unsafe.

At a stock-funds boundary, stage validated simulation intent, pause advancement if the main-thread debit cannot be committed/reconciled, and persist receipt/status before exposing committed state. External observations continue queuing while blocked. Never simulate successful paid departures/rewards from stale funds or skip time to bypass a failed debit. Phase 3/3A must support deterministic pure replacement events/order and saved backlog contracts before Phase 4 attaches live capture. Tests: pre-event crew/orbit history, equal-UT causal replacements vs arrivals/commands, observations during budget exhaustion/funds pause, unknown-UT suspension, duplicate/conflicting events, save-mid-backlog and rollback replacing the abandoned journal. These pure fixtures are not KSP callback verification.

