Write scenario schema 6. Required sections: COLONIES, DEPOTS, BUILDINGS, STARTER_KITS, CONSTRUCTION_JOBS, RESOURCE_STORAGE, BATCHES, VEINS, FUEL_RESERVOIRS, HULL_ORDERS, FUEL_INVENTORY, ROUTES, TRANSPORT_VEHICLES, TRANSPORT_SLOTS, CONSIGNMENTS, FLIGHT_LEGS, SHIPMENTS, CAMPAIGN, BLUEPRINTS, REWARD_RECEIPTS, DELIVERY_CLAIMS, COMMAND_RECEIPTS, SETTINGS, SIMULATION_CURSOR, RESERVATIONS, DISPATCH_POLICIES, BERTH_BOOKINGS, PARKING_ENTITLEMENTS, METRIC_WINDOWS. Persist source provenance, reservations, partial arrivals, vehicle turnaround jobs, maintenance reservations, objective counters, environment sample interval, and last fault window. Also persist vehicle location and onboard propellant, maneuver cursor, refuel/berth reservations, commissioning jobs, orbit elements/epoch/validity, template snapshot, protected fuel, consignment eligibility/handoff history and physical depot mass cache. Also persist kit receipts tied to anchor identity, remaining/packed tokens with machine state, storage resource/tier, material-paid basis, escrow balances, completed module structure mass and build/upgrade queue timestamps.

Schema 6 is a future live scenario contract, not the pure MaterialBookSnapshot version 2 or pending WorldSnapshot version 1. Add explicit versioned subsections for EXTERNAL_EVENT_JOURNAL, VERIFIED_SNAPSHOTS and OWNED_POWER: target/cursor, equal-UT phase frontier, capture/apply sequence/receipts, old/new snapshot references and timestamp quality; core capacity/charge/generator condition/fault/output profile, registration grant receipt and priority; partial cargo/pump/setup work and energy/funds receipts plus source return-capacity; logical campaign progress/reward identity, acceptance/binding epochs, obsolete-claim release receipts, production/cycle/throughput counters and retry windows. Do not renumber already accepted pure formats just to match scenario schema. Phase 4 owns live serializers and migration, later owning phases extend their sections with fixtures.

Use Guid D strings for GUID references; internal stable IDs are canonical strings. Invariant round-trip double formatting. Parse finite values only. Missing optional fields use documented defaults. Duplicate IDs or conflicting claims quarantine the affected record with a visible diagnostic; never silently duplicate inventory. Save/load is atomic from the simulator's perspective; do not serialize half of a departure transaction.

Migration: schema 1 legacy routes/manifests are not compatible inventory; preserve the original nodes in LEGACY_BACKUP and disable them with a warning. Schema 2 colonies retain goods, provenance, ground depletion, converter condition and location. Create owned starter power and battery using variant defaults; do not steal spacecraft EC. Old shipments retain saved departure/arrival times and cargo; create reservations where capacity permits, else visible waitingForSpace state. Old seed/debug ore is campaign-ineligible. Do not invent campaign completion or infer blueprints from inventory. Building funds-paid defaults to zero for old saves, preventing unearned refunds. Document these deliberate rules.

Schema 3 migration preserves old in-flight shipments as legacy funded flights that arrive once on their old timers; they do not become free fuel or reusable located vehicles. Preserve old return-slot availability until its pending flight closes, then disable old service rules until the player commissions fuel-aware vehicles. Do not retroactively charge fuel for already paid flights. Give existing colonies the new empty fuel/feed capacities and unlocked baseline pump/refinery, never a free propellant grant. Campaign completions/blueprints remain, while new depot quests are offered according to their prerequisites. Legacy cargo keeps original dispatch eligibility and may complete a previously accepted quest only under its preserved acceptance rule.

Schema 4 migration preserves existing stored amounts and old paid capacity as explicit legacy capacity grants per resource, rather than shrinking a full store below its content. Replace new-building catalog options with the resource-specific recipes; do not multiply legacy caps. Existing buildings retain condition/fault and funds-paid history, but their old funds-only purchase never becomes refundable Metal/Parts. Existing kits did not exist: give each surviving hab only starter pieces not already represented by its existing matching machinery/storage, record the reconciliation receipt, and never grant another set on reload. Existing integral hab fuel buffers keep their legacy amount/cap until emptied; later expansion must use dedicated storage. New habs use the small integrated capacities. Preserve campaign completion IDs and remap old silo/fuel tank blueprint rewards to advanced dedicated storage/fuel upgrade as appropriate. No old save loses inventory just because capacity rules changed.

Schema 5 migration retains quantities, provenance, live jobs, fuel plans, inventories, starter receipts and capacities. Consolidate existing job/shipment claims into ReservationBook without reserving escrow/onboard matter a second time; conflicting claims quarantine affected jobs and expose recovery. Add default dispatch policy/timestamps without replaying departures. Existing located depot vehicles need deterministic parking/berth reconciliation; preserve excess occupants in visible legacy-overcapacity state, block new occupancy until resolved, and never delete vehicles or cargo. Existing active handling finishes under its retained booking. Create no battery, fuel or blueprint grant. Existing c03 battery rewards map to advanced-battery-bank. Preserve stored condition but use the new configured default wear for future operation unless the save has an explicit custom wear rate; never retroactively repair equipment. Initialize bounded metrics from migration UT without inventing historical utilization. Persist reconciliation receipt so repeat load cannot duplicate claims.

For a future unknown schema, preserve its complete raw node and open Lodeworks read-only with an incompatibility warning; do not save a reduced subset over it. Quickload must replace all caches, claimed IDs, and queued events with the loaded snapshot. Recovery/destruction only deletes the colony after verifying the anchor is genuinely gone; vessel GUID changes are not destruction.

Receipt logs may compact only when no active contract/shipments can reference them. Preserve aggregate completed campaign/reward flags permanently. Never truncate deduplication records that still guard a live transaction.

## 16 Technical standards and diagnostics

- Pure simulation references neither Unity nor KSP; no I/O, mutable statics, DateTime, random APIs, or logging framework.
- KSP callbacks are main-thread only. No async/worker-thread access to game objects.
- Resource simulation never runs in PartModule.FixedUpdate/OnFixedUpdate. One scenario driver advances world UT.
- [KSPField(isPersistant = true)] is spelled as the game requires. Verify callback signatures rather than guessing.
- Named event handlers; every subscription has removal. No writes to part prefabs, global resource definitions, or other mods' state. Explicit stock-fuel bridge writes use the verified broker only; fuel in stock tanks must never also be in the ledger.
- Boundary exceptions log [Lodeworks], suspend affected subsystem for the scene, preserve save data, and show the player an actionable diagnostic. Do not silently persist every building disabled after an adapter error.
- Transition logs: deploy, command failure, departure, arrival, lost cargo, fault, maintenance start/end, campaign completion, reward grant, migration and catch-up warning. Avoid inventory spam inside ticks.
- Optional diagnostic export: UT, colonyId, buildingId, reason, input/output deltas, power balance, shipmentId, routeId, contractId. Include a compact event ring buffer for debugging; export only on explicit request/debug option.
- Avoid LINQ and avoidable allocations in the hot simulation driver. Pool buffers where useful; do not make impossible zero-allocation promises while allocating result dictionaries every tick.
- Test pure logic automatically. Add adapter integration checks for state ownership, scene changes, contract registration, unlock enforcement, and funds transactions. Manual KSP verification remains required.
- Gitignore bin/obj, local paths, game DLLs and PDBs. Ship MIT license for original code/configs. No bundled MiniAVC, Harmony, Module Manager, or runtime NuGet dependency.

Settings are per-save for gameplay: RateMultiplier=1, WearMultiplier=1, FaultsEnabled=True, AutoMaintenance=True, HardMaintenance=False, CatchupEnabled=True, SandboxCampaign=False. Visual toggles/debug export are installation preferences. With CatchupEnabled=False, intentionally advance inactive cursors without production and show background simulation disabled; this is an explicit difficulty deviation from partition invariance. It must not duplicate pending arrivals or campaign rewards.

Target performance: 20 colonies, 200 buildings, 100 routes, and 100 shipments remain responsive on a stock-capable PC. Profile steady state separately from catch-up. Respect the 4 ms catch-up budget, show backlog, and prioritize correctness over hiding delayed simulation. Do not assert performance solely from absence of logs.

