### 17.2 Independent low/typical/high balance fixtures and warnings

Phases 5/6A/7/8/9/10A/16/20 own numerical fixtures and game calibration, respectively extraction, electric/power, fuel, flight, service, construction pacing, graph/campaign and long-run balance. Test joint startup/export/expansion/fuel/maintenance budgets, not each system with free prefilled tanks. Use abundance 0.05/0.50/1.00 and grade 0.25/0.60/0.95 as independent low/typical/high envelopes, not assertions that the hash produces these together at every site. Test no-Engineer 0.50 and Engineer levels 0/1 (0.60/0.75); no crew means no industry, autonomous power/handling unaffected.

Independent material-only reference (not a completed simulation): one extra basic bank 60 Metal + Metal bin 60 + first shop 120 + produce 100 Parts at 2.5 Metal/Part + 20 Supplies at 0.4 Metal/Supply + one 20-Supplies service = **506 Metal**. Reserve ore export before smelting. Fuel, hull launches, further capacity and flight energy must be added to that bundle, not claimed included.

| Body / export held aside | Low capacity / potential Metal after export | Typical capacity / potential Metal | High capacity / potential Metal |
|---|---|---|---|
| Kerbin / 150 ore (c01 mining does not consume its 50; c02 consumes 100, this fixture conservatively holds 150) | 1000 / 46.75 | 10000 / 1300.20 | 20000 / 4148.65 |
| Mun / 100 ore | 600 / 43.75 | 6000 / 1239.00 | 12000 / 3956.75 |
| Minmus / 75 ore | 250 / 24.0625 | 2500 / 800.25 | 5000 / 2573.3125 |

Metal = (capacity-export)*grade*bodyYield. Freeze the additional adversarial Minmus vector: **20000*0.25*0.05 = 250 ore; 250-75 = 175; 175*0.95*0.55 = 91.4375 Metal < 120-Metal shop**, even at maximum grade. This is a one-site balance warning, not a proven global progression deadlock: shop production elsewhere, other cells/locations and optional imports remain valid. At low grade, the 506-Metal bundle needs 3680 smelted Minmus ore plus 75 export, so at least 16 independent 250-unit cells are required in this conservative uniform-low envelope (ceil(3755/250)); more habs in one shared cell do not add deposit capacity. Show these estimates, never promise every site can support every stage.

At verified default densities, 1 t LF/OX = 90 LF + 110 OX. Refinery needs 500 nominal active seconds: either 125 ore (ore mode, 7500 power, plus extraction energy) or 250 feed (feed mode, 5000 power plus 3000 pump energy). A depot pump needs 100 power/t; 1 t/min uses 100/60 power/s, below small-core 20 generation, and 1200 setup power needs 60 seconds' generation but still 600 powered setup seconds. These independent bounds justify empty-core bootstrap power, not a claim that ascent fuel fits every vehicle or that a whole route costs only 1 t.

For each fixture compute needed ore = exports + Metal/(grade*yield) + ore-mode fuel ore; account separately finite feed for feed mode, discarded slag, Parts/Supplies conversions, deck/storage construction, available owned generation and electric full-cycle energy. Extraction time to remove Q<C is C/(0.5*crew)*ln(C/(C-Q)); processing time is ore/(0.5*crew), with actual simultaneous power/storage contention and exponential depletion. Report remaining/C, residual rule, time-to-depletion, service threshold 325000 operating seconds, Supplies demand and emergency visits. Tests must independently calculate expectations and compare simulation/quotes; nominal sums are not game pacing proof.

Independently audit progression: baseline makes Metal/banks/storage/c01; c01 enables c02 electric service; c02 enables light shuttle/lifter/tug/small core and feed-fueled d01/c03; c03 enables capsule/c03 battery before d02, which requires d01+c03. c04 enables Metal-only shop before c05 Parts; c05+d02 enables c06's Supplies/Metal cycles using existing vehicles; c06 heavy lander precedes c07; c07 generator precedes c08's baseline-vehicle fuel cycles; c08 precedes c09's retryable throughput; later unchanged branches retain prerequisites. Test actual configured DAG, objective semantics and role/mass capacity, including dry tug hull delivery, rather than relying on this textual audit. No Parts expansion is needed to unlock d01/d02; optional imports remain optional.

